# Project-curated cppcheck suppressions for pgxbackup.
# Format: <check-id>[:<file>[:<line>]] -- one per line. Lines starting with # are comments.
# Each suppression must have a rationale comment immediately above. Do not add suppressions to silence real findings; if a
# finding is real, fix it (or use an inline `// cppcheck-suppress <id>` annotation at the call site).

# unassignedVariable (global): cppcheck cannot see across the FUNCTION_TEST_BEGIN / FUNCTION_LOG_BEGIN / FUNCTION_TEST_VOID
# macro family declared in src/common/debug.h. Each macro expands to a state machine that declares a sentinel local
# (FUNCTION_TEST_BEGIN_exists) consumed by the matching FUNCTION_TEST_RETURN / FUNCTION_LOG_RETURN. cppcheck only sees the
# declaration, not the cross-macro consumption, so reports the sentinel as unassigned. The pattern frames hundreds of functions
# across the codebase, so this suppression is global rather than path-scoped.
unassignedVariable

# unreadVariable (one site): manifest.c records a memory-context size before a loop and consumes it after the loop, both inside
# matching #ifdef DEBUG_MEM blocks. cppcheck does not follow preprocessor branches and so reports the assignment as dead. The
# variable IS read in the matching DEBUG_MEM block further down -- search for sizeBegin in src/info/manifest/manifest.c.
unreadVariable:src/info/manifest/manifest.c:247

# nullPointerRedundantCheck (global): cppcheck cannot tell that FUNCTION_TEST_PARAM_P / FUNCTION_LOG_PARAM_P do NOT null-check
# their argument -- they are conditional logging hooks that compile out in production builds. cppcheck's flow analysis treats
# the macro as if it could short-circuit on a null parameter, then flags any subsequent pointer dereference as either redundant
# (if the macro guards) or unsafe (if it doesn't). Same root cause as the unassignedVariable suppression above.
nullPointerRedundantCheck

# unknownEvaluationOrder (global): cppcheck flags C99 compound literals in function arguments
# (e.g., foo(bar, (struct Param){.dummy = false})) as having ambiguous evaluation order. The C99/C11 standards explicitly
# define the evaluation of compound literals at function-call sites; cppcheck's analysis is overly conservative here. The
# pgxbackup codebase uses compound literals pervasively as a variadic-function-argument idiom (see CODING.md). 54 findings
# of this category were inspected and confirmed false-positives; suppressing globally rather than 54 inline annotations.
unknownEvaluationOrder
